I am preparing for a Senior Machine Learning / Applied AI/ML Engineer interview and want to learn PySpark from first principles over 20 days.

MY CURRENT LEVEL
Python: 9/10
Pandas: 9/10
PySpark: 1/10
SQL: beginner/intermediate unless told otherwise
AWS Glue: no prior hands-on experience
Databricks: no/limited prior experience
Spark: assume essentially no conceptual foundation

I am strong in Python and Pandas — don't spend time teaching basic Python. Use my existing Python/Pandas knowledge as a bridge to explain how PySpark is different, but do NOT imply Spark is "Pandas but bigger." It is a fundamentally different distributed execution model.

PRIMARY OBJECTIVE
My goal is not to become a Spark platform administrator or specialist. My goal is to become a Senior ML Engineer who can:
1. Write PySpark code and understand what it's doing
2. Explain why PySpark is useful for large ML datasets
3. Build distributed ETL/data-preparation pipelines
4. Prepare large datasets for ML training and inference
5. Explain Spark concepts at a senior interview level
6. Troubleshoot common Spark performance problems
7. Design an ML data pipeline using PySpark
8. Explain how PySpark fits into AWS Glue, S3, Iceberg, Snowflake, and SageMaker
9. Eventually map these Spark concepts to Databricks

Primary use case: PySpark for preparing data for SageMaker/ML models. Spark MLlib is NOT the focus.

ARCHITECTURE CONTEXT
Representative pipeline: Vendor/FTP/external source -> S3 -> PySpark -> clean/validate/standardize -> Iceberg tables -> SQL joins/transformations -> ML-ready dataset -> SageMaker (training/inference). Snowflake is a possible downstream system.

TECHNOLOGY DISTINCTIONS (do not blur these)
S3: cloud object storage / data lake
PySpark: distributed data-processing engine
AWS Glue: managed AWS environment for running Spark ETL jobs, integrates with AWS services
Iceberg: table format providing table semantics over object storage
Snowflake: cloud data warehouse / analytical platform
Databricks: managed Spark/data/ML platform
SageMaker: ML training/inference/platform services
I want to be able to say in an interview: "PySpark is the distributed processing engine. Glue or Databricks provides the managed environment in which that Spark processing can run."

TEACHING PHILOSOPHY
Do not assume I understand: distributed computing, partitions, executors, shuffle, lazy evaluation, Spark jobs/stages/tasks, broadcast joins, data skew. Build the mental model before terminology. Use Pandas analogies where useful, but always name the important difference (e.g. Pandas processes on one machine; Spark distributes across partitions/executors).

20-DAY CURRICULUM
Days 1-5 (fundamentals): Day1 What is Spark/PySpark (Spark vs Pandas, SparkSession, DataFrames, driver, executors, basic partitions, transformations vs actions) / Day2 DataFrames and schemas (rows, columns, schema, dtypes, show, printSchema, select) / Day3 Basic transformations (filter, where, select, withColumn, drop, alias, cast) / Day4 Feature/data prep (when, otherwise, null handling, type conversion, standardization, feature creation) / Day5 Aggregations (groupBy, agg, count, sum, avg, min, max) tied to ML feature engineering

Days 6-10 (execution model): Day6 Spark SQL (both DataFrame API and SQL, comfortable switching between them) / Day7 Lazy evaluation (transformations vs actions, logical execution, why Spark doesn't execute immediately) / Day8 Partitions (what/why, parallel processing, partition count, basic repartition) / Day9 Narrow vs wide transformations (narrow: filter/select/withColumn; wide: groupBy/join/distinct/orderBy) / Day10 Shuffle (what it is, why it happens, why expensive, relation to partitions, basic troubleshooting)

Days 11-15 (real ML data engineering): Day11 Joins (inner/left/right/full/semi/anti, ML-style datasets) / Day12 Broadcast joins (why it exists, when appropriate, limitations, broadcast(), reasoning not just "small table = broadcast") / Day13 Data skew (hot keys, uneven partitions, detection, mitigation) / Day14 repartition() vs coalesce() (increase/decrease partitions, shuffle implications, output file implications, when to use each) / Day15 Error handling and retry (Python exceptions, Spark task/job retries, pipeline-level retries, bad records, validation failures, partial failures, idempotency, restartability, avoiding duplicate outputs — distinguish task retry vs job retry vs pipeline retry)

Days 16-20 (production ML + AWS): Day16 Parquet + S3 (CSV vs Parquet, columnar storage, schema, compression, read/write S3, why Parquet for analytical processing) / Day17 Predicate pushdown + partition pruning + file sizing (partition columns, file sizes, small-file problem) / Day18 AWS Glue from first principles (Glue job, Spark runtime, Data Catalog, bookmarks, job parameters, how PySpark runs in Glue vs local Spark — I have never used Glue) / Day19 Iceberg + Snowflake + Databricks (why Iceberg, snapshots, schema evolution, basic partitioning, ETL into Iceberg, SQL after tables exist, Snowflake as downstream ML consumption, how Glue/Spark concepts translate to Databricks) / Day20 Senior ML pipeline interview simulation (realistic 2-5TB/day vendor transaction pipeline: ingest, validate, clean, dedupe, standardize, join dimensions, generate ML features, produce training/inference dataset, handle failures/retries/skew/large+small files, write to Iceberg, feed SageMaker — defend the architecture)

UDF / BATCH INFERENCE REQUIREMENT
Cover Python UDFs, Pandas UDFs, vectorized processing, batch inference, large-scale ML inference as an ML engineering topic, not just API syntax. Explain: why plain Python UDFs are problematic, where the model is loaded, how executors access it, why loading a model per row is bad, batching, model reuse, memory implications, serialization, when Pandas UDF makes sense vs when SageMaker Batch Transform or another inference architecture is more appropriate. Include a syntax example like:
@pandas_udf("double")
def predict(features: pd.Series) -> pd.Series:
    return model.predict(features)
but only after explaining the reasoning above.

CODING ENVIRONMENT
Start local: SparkSession.builder.master("local[*]").getOrCreate() — no AWS/Databricks account needed for early lessons. Later, explain what changes moving to AWS Glue and Databricks.

LESSON FORMAT — Format B: Full Lesson, every lesson contains:
1. Concept (first principles)
2. Mental model (simple picture of what Spark is doing)
3. Code (runnable PySpark, realistic ML/data-engineering examples over toy examples)
4. Walk through the code (explain important lines)
5. Quiz (mix of conceptual, code-reading, "what happens internally", interview-style questions)
